[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] competed for five trophies in the 1966–67 season and the club won all of them: the Scottish League, the Scottish Cup, the Scottish League Cup, the Glasgow Cup, and the European Cup, and completed the only ever European [[List of association football teams to have won four or more trophies in one season#Five titles in one season|Quintuple]].<ref name="CFCHistory">{{cite news|url=http://www.celticfc.net/about_briefhistory |title=Celtic:Brief History |publisher=Celtic FC |access-date=12 July 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111119014926/http://www.celticfc.net/about_briefhistory |archive-date=19 November 2011 }}</ref> However, their European Cup victory from this season qualified them for the [[Intercontinental Cup (1960–2004)|World Championship]] the following season, which they went on to lose in a playoff to [[Racing Club de Avellaneda|Racing]]. Over the course of this season, Celtic scored a world record 196 goals in the major competitions they took part in.<ref name="goals196">{{cite web|title=World Record and Statistics|url=http://xtrahistory.blogspot.co.uk/2014/04/Record-Statistics.html|website=World Football Historic Center|access-date=17 June 2014}}</ref>

The team from this season are commonly known as the [[Lisbon Lions]], because the [[1967 European Cup final|European Cup final]] was held in Lisbon.

==Season overview==

Season 1966–67 is considered Celtic's [[annus mirabilis]]. The club won every competition they entered: the [[Scottish Football League|Scottish League]], the [[Scottish Cup]], the [[Scottish League Cup]], the [[Glasgow Cup]], and the [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]]; scoring a world record total of 196 goals in the process.<ref name="goals196" />

The League Cup was the first trophy to be won that season, courtesy of a 1–0 win on 29 October 1966 over [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] in the final.<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic fight in final|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/AlI2v8uzdNzMWiAJCrOPTA118142|access-date=20 January 2014|newspaper=The Times|date=31 October 1966|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181003015905/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/AlI2v8uzdNzMWiAJCrOPTA118142|archive-date=3 October 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref> The Glasgow Cup was secured a week later when Celtic beat [[Partick Thistle F.C.|Partick Thistle]] 4–0; Stevie Chalmers opening the scoring, and Bobby Lennox scoring a hat-trick.<ref>{{cite news|last=Edwards|first=Glyn|title=Thistle no match for Celtic in Glasgow Cup Final|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=3dw9AAAAIBAJ&pg=1656%2C1217839|access-date=20 January 2014|newspaper=The Glasgow Herald|date=8 November 1966|page=6}}</ref> Celtic's progression to the Scottish Cup was relatively straightforward aside from being taken to replay in the semi-final by [[Clyde F.C.|Clyde]].<ref name="sc67">{{harvnb|Wilson|1988|p=134}}</ref> On 29 April 1967 Celtic met [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] in the final, and two [[Willie Wallace]] goals eased Celtic to a 2–0 win.<ref name="sc67" /> The league campaign proved to be a more tightly contested affair as, despite Celtic only losing twice, with two games remaining Rangers were still in contention.  Celtic's penultimate league fixture was against Rangers at Ibrox, with a draw required to clinch the title.  A brace by [[Jimmy Johnstone]] gave Celtic a 2–2 draw and the championship.<ref name="sc67" />

Celtic's European Cup campaign in 1966–67 was their first ever participation in Europe's premier club tournament. [[FC Zürich]] and [[FC Nantes|Nantes]] were comfortably disposed of in the first two rounds (5–0 and 6–2 on aggregate respectively).<ref>{{cite web|title=Season 1966-67|url=http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro.html|website=European Cup History|access-date=11 December 2016}}{{Dead link|date=August 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes}}</ref> The quarter final in March 1967 pitched Celtic against the [[Yugoslavia|Yugoslav]] champions, [[FK Vojvodina|Vojvodina]]. The Yugoslav side won the first leg in [[Novi Sad]] 1–0. The return match in Glasgow proved to be a fraught affair.  The Yugoslavs defended resolutely and threatened on the counter-attack, but Celtic levelled the tie on aggregate in the second half with a goal by Stevie Chalmers.<ref>{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|p=235}}</ref> Celtic pressed for a winner, but Vojvodina defended well and the tie looked like a play-off in neutral Rotterdam would be required.  However, in the final minute [[Billy McNeill]] headed in a [[Charlie Gallagher (association footballer)|Charlie Gallacher]] cross to see Celtic progress to the semi-final.  Celtic now faced [[Czechoslovakia|Czechoslovak]] side [[Dukla Prague]]. This time the first leg of the tie took place in Glasgow, with Celtic winning 3–1 courtesy of goals from Jimmy Johnstone and a Willie Wallace brace.<ref>{{harvnb|Wilson|1988|p=136}}</ref> In respect of his opponents' quality, manager [[Jock Stein]] set up Celtic to be ultra-defensive for the second leg and forsake - temporarily - their philosophy of attacking football.<ref>{{harvnb|Wilson|1988|pp=136–137}}</ref> The tactics worked as Celtic secured a 0–0 draw to put them in the final.  However, Stein was almost apologetic about the manner of Celtic's success in that game and he felt uncomfortable in later years discussing the matter.<ref>{{harvnb|Wilson|1988|p=137}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|p=236}}</ref>{{football squad on pitch|align=right
| GK = [[Ronnie Simpson|'''Simpson''']]
| RB = [[Jim Craig (Scottish footballer)|'''Craig''']]
| RCB = [[Billy McNeill|'''McNeill(C)''']]
| LCB = [[John Clark (footballer born 1941)|'''Clark''']]
| LB = [[Tommy Gemmell|'''Gemmell''']]
| RCM = [[Bertie Auld|'''Auld''']]
| LCM = [[Bobby Murdoch|'''Murdoch''']]
| RW = [[Jimmy Johnstone|'''Johnstone''']]
| RCF = [[Stevie Chalmers|'''Chalmers''']]
| LCF = [[Willie Wallace|'''Wallace''']]
| LW = [[bobby Lennox|'''Lennox''']]
| caption = [[1967 European Cup Final]] line-up<br />(The Lisbon Lions)
}}

The final saw Celtic play [[Internazionale Milano F.C.|Inter Milan]], with the match taking place at the [[Estádio Nacional]] on the outskirts of [[Lisbon]] on 25 May 1967.  Celtic fell a goal behind after only seven minutes, [[Jim Craig (Scottish footballer)|Jim Craig]] adjudged to have fouled [[Renato Cappellini]] in the penalty box and [[Sandro Mazzola]] converting the resultant penalty.<ref name="67match">{{cite news|last1=Barham|first1=Albert|title=Relentless attack captures European Cup|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/1967/may/25/championsleague.sport|access-date=27 June 2014|work=The Guardian|date=26 May 1967}}</ref> Celtic swept into constant attack after that but found Inter goalkeeper [[Giuliano Sarti]] in outstanding form.<ref name="67match" /> With 63 minutes played, after incessant pressure, Celtic finally equalised when [[Tommy Gemmell]] scored with a powerful 25-yard shot.<ref name="67match" /> The balance of play remained the same with Inter defending deeply against sustained Celtic attacking.  With about five minutes remaining, a long-range shot from [[Bobby Murdoch]] was diverted by [[Stevie Chalmers]] past a wrong-footed Sarti.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Forsyth|first1=Roddy|title=Murdoch the true Lionheart|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/scottish-premier/3005452/Murdoch-the-true-Lionheart.html|work=The Telegraph|access-date=27 June 2014|date=15 May 2001}}</ref> It proved to be the winning goal and thus Celtic became the first British team, and the first from outside Spain, Portugal or Italy to win the competition.<ref name="67match" />

Jock Stein commented after the match,

{{cquote|Winning was important, but it was the way that we won that has filled me with satisfaction.  We did it by playing football; pure, beautiful, inventive football. There was not a negative thought in our heads.<ref>{{cite news|title=25 May - 1967: Celtic win European Cup|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/may/25/newsid_5071000/5071020.stm|publisher=BBC On This Day|access-date=22 October 2013|date=25 May 1967}}</ref>}}

Celtic are one of only two clubs to have won the trophy with a team composed entirely of players from the club's home country; all of the players in the side were born within 30 miles of Celtic Park in Glasgow, and they subsequently became known as the '[[Lisbon Lions]]'.<ref>{{cite book |url=https://archive.org/details/nowyouknowsoccer0000lenn |url-access=registration |quote=now you know soccer who were the lisbon lions. |page=[https://archive.org/details/nowyouknowsoccer0000lenn/page/143 143] |title=Now You Know Soccer |author=Doug Lennox |website=Dundurn Press |isbn=978-1-55488-416-2 |year=2009}}</ref> The entire east stand at [[Celtic Park]] is dedicated to [[The Lisbon Lions]],<ref>{{cite web|first=Jim|last=Reynolds |url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/spl/aberdeen/fans-will-now-roar-from-the-lisbon-lions-stand-1.251751 |title=Fans will now roar from the Lisbon Lions Stand |website=Herald Scotland |date=28 January 2000 |access-date=5 August 2013}}</ref> and the west stand to Jock Stein.<ref>{{cite news|first=Simon|last=Buckland|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-leonhardsen-too-smart-for-celtic-1170680.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-leonhardsen-too-smart-for-celtic-1170680.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Leonhardsen too smart for Celtic |work=The Independent|date=9 August 1998 |access-date=5 August 2013}}{{cbignore}}</ref> The sight of captain [[Billy McNeill]] holding aloft the European Cup in the Estádio Nacional has become one of the iconic images of Scottish football, immortalized in a bronze statue of McNeill outside Parkhead stadium in 2015, created by [[John McKenna (sculptor)]].<ref name="bil50">{{cite web|title=The 50 Greatest Scottish Footballers - Billy McNeill|url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/football/the-50-greatest-scottish-footballers-day-5-x.20578473|website=Herald Online|access-date=11 March 2014|date=22 March 2013}}</ref><ref name="bilstat">{{cite web|last=Burns|first=Scott|title=Time to build a statue for Hoops legend McNeill|url=http://www.express.co.uk/sport/football/443095/Time-to-build-a-statue-for-Hoops-legend-McNeill|website=Scottish Express|access-date=11 March 2014|date=14 November 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/may/24/celtic-lisbon-lions-50-greatest-success-story-scottish-sport|title=The Lisbon Lions 50 years later: still the greatest story ever told in Scottish sport|date=24 May 2017|work=The Guardian|access-date=25 May 2017}}</ref>

Two weeks later, on 7 June 1967, Celtic played [[Real Madrid]] in a testimonial match for the now retired [[Alfredo Di Stefano]].  In front of over 100,000 fans at the [[Santiago Bernabéu Stadium|Bernabéu Stadium]], the sides engaged in a keenly fought contest which saw [[Bertie Auld]] and Real Madrid's [[Amancio Amaro|Amancio]] sent off.  Di Stefano played for the first 15 minutes, but it was Jimmy Johnstone who stole the show with an exhilarating performance that had even the Spanish supporters chanting "Olé!" throughout the game in appreciation of his skill.  Johnstone capped an outstanding performance by playing the pass to Bobby Lennox for the only goal in a 1–0 win for Celtic.<ref>{{cite news|last=McMillan|first=Anna|title=The Alfredo Di Stefano Trophy|newspaper=The Celtic View|date=16 November 2005}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=The Full Story: 1967, Alfredo Di Stéfano's Testimonial |url=http://programmes.stv.tv/the-football-years/series-1/1967/227825-the-full-story-alfredo-di-stfanos-testimonial-1967/ |publisher=STV The Football Years |access-date=22 October 2013 |date=12 February 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131022015651/http://programmes.stv.tv/the-football-years/series-1/1967/227825-the-full-story-alfredo-di-stfanos-testimonial-1967/ |archive-date=22 October 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=967-06-07: Real Madrid 0-1 Celtic, Testimonial - Alfredo Di Stefano|url=http://www.thecelticwiki.com/page/1967-06-07%3A+Real+Madrid+0-1+Celtic,+Testimonial+-+Alfredo+Di+Stefano|website=The Celtic Wiki|access-date=22 October 2013|archive-date=11 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201111232954/http://www.thecelticwiki.com/page/1967-06-07%3A+Real+Madrid+0-1+Celtic,+Testimonial+-+Alfredo+Di+Stefano|url-status=dead}}</ref>

